            <ArticleTitle>Dietary prevention of coronary artery disease. How to help patients modify eating habits and reduce cholesterol.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>243-50, 257-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The prevalence of high serum cholesterol levels and the associated epidemic of coronary artery disease in our society are largely the by-products of a maladaptive diet. Dietary modification is the logical and effective approach to this problem for most patients. In general, dietary therapy consists of a reduction in the intake of saturated fatty acids, cholesterol, and excess calories. Many specific dietary modifications, including increased intake of omega-9 and omega-3 fatty acids and soluble fiber, may also help reduce cholesterol levels and prevent coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
